<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  W4-A0 (#234) - the WiX MSI skeleton. Extended by W4-A2 with the W0 §4 SCM
  failure actions.

  W4-A0's claim: a WiX project in-tree produces an MSI that installs the
  accepted win-x64 server layout, creates the service `Tesserafin` with the
  `service` switch and the `docs/distribution/W0-windows-server.md` §4 argument
  list, and uninstalls the service and the binaries while leaving the state
  directories behind.

  W4-A2 adds ONE thing to that, and nothing else: the installed service carries
  the §4 recovery policy - restart after 60 s on the first and the second
  failure, no action on the third - and the hosted proof reads that policy back
  out of the Service Control Manager after the install rather than out of this
  file. See `docs/distribution/W4-A2-service-recovery.md`.

  W4-A2-R1 (#234) changed HOW, and only how. The core
  `ServiceConfigFailureActions` element this slice first authored writes the
  `MsiServiceConfigFailureActions` table, and `MsiConfigureServices` answered
  MSI error 1939 for it under InstallFinalize, rolling the install back to 1603.
  The owner ruling authorised `util:ServiceConfig` from
  `WixToolset.Util.wixext` in its place - the first WiX extension this packaging
  takes a dependency on, pinned by exact version on the `wix` command line in
  `ci/windows/w4/build-msi.ps1`. The POLICY is unchanged and so is what is
  graded; the core element is gone from this file entirely.

  W4-A3 (#234) adds the W0 §9.3 ACL contract, and only that. It is authored as
  SDDL on the Windows Installer 5.0 `MsiLockPermissionsEx` table, which WiX
  writes from the CORE `PermissionEx` element, and since W4-A3-R2 there is one
  descriptor per directory rather than one per tree:

    `%ProgramData%\Tesserafin\`  a PROTECTED descriptor: Administrators and
                                 SYSTEM Full, `NT SERVICE\Tesserafin` Modify,
                                 and no unprivileged identity at all.

    `…\Tesserafin\Server` and    the same descriptor, authored on each. W4-A3
    its config, data, cache      relied on the OICI ACEs above to carry it down
    and log directories          and run 34502732425 measured that they do not:
                                 the DACL is written PROTECTED and without
                                 SE_DACL_AUTO_INHERITED, the auto-inherit pass
                                 never runs, and every one of the four came back
                                 carrying `%ProgramData%`'s own default with its
                                 `Users` write bits intact. A grant is only
                                 where it is authored.

    INSTALLFOLDER                an UNPROTECTED descriptor. The row W0 §9.3 is
                                 about is `NT SERVICE\Tesserafin`, read and
                                 execute, no write bit at all, because the
                                 service must not be able to rewrite its own
                                 binaries or its own FFmpeg. Administrators and
                                 SYSTEM Full and `Users` read and execute are
                                 authored beside it: W4-A3-R1 measured that a
                                 descriptor applied through this table becomes
                                 the object's WHOLE DACL, so the rows a
                                 %ProgramFiles% directory already has do not
                                 survive it and have to be stated. `P` is still
                                 not asked for, because W0 §9.3 breaks
                                 inheritance at `%ProgramData%\Tesserafin\` and
                                 nowhere else.

  The account is named by SID, not by name: `NT SERVICE\Tesserafin` is a virtual
  service account whose SID is `S-1-5-80` followed by the SHA-1 of the upper-case
  UTF-16LE service name as five little-endian DWORDs, so it is the same value on
  every machine and it exists before the service does. SDDL takes SIDs;
  `ci/windows/w4/msi-controls.py` recomputes this one from the service name and
  reddens the authoring if it does not match, because a well-formed SDDL naming
  some other SID installs perfectly and grants nobody anything.

  The rights are hexadecimal access masks, never the SDDL generic aliases: the
  hosted proof reads them back through `Get-Acl`, which reports
  `FileSystemRights` as the file-specific mask, and a generic alias would come
  back as a number no predicate could compare.

  The extension question the W4-A3 ruling asks was settled by measurement rather
  than preference. `WixToolset.Util.wixext` 6.0.2 is already pinned by W4-A2, and
  its `util:PermissionEx` cannot express this contract: the compiler answers
  `error WIX0004: The PermissionEx element contains an unexpected attribute
  'Sddl'` and `error WIX0010: The PermissionEx/@User attribute was not found; it
  is required`, and it accepts no `Protected`, `DenyInheritance`, `NoInheritance`
  or `ReplaceExisting` attribute either. It can add an ACE; it cannot break
  inheritance. The core element needs no extension at all, so no second extension
  is taken.

  The core `Permission` element is NOT used and must not be: it writes the
  `LockPermissions` table, Windows Installer refuses a package that carries both
  tables, and `LockPermissions` always discards inherited permissions, which
  would take the choice this slice is about away from the authoring.

  W4-A4 (#234) adds NO product behaviour at all. `MajorUpgrade` has stood in this
  file since W4-A0 and W4-A4 is the slice that EXERCISES it: install one package,
  write sentinels into the four `%ProgramData%` state directories, install a
  second package built from THIS authoring with the same `UpgradeCode` and a
  higher `Version`, and read back that the binaries under INSTALLFOLDER are the
  second package's, that the sentinels are still there byte for byte, that the
  service is still registered and still Stopped with the §4 binPath, and that the
  §9.3 descriptors still hold. The two packages differ by a `Version` the builder
  derives from the SAME `SharedVersion.cs` read it has always used - see
  `-PatchBump` in `ci/windows/w4/build-msi.ps1` - and by nothing else. No
  `SharedVersion.cs` edit, no second product, no new component, no moved
  failure-action or SDDL attribute value.

  Two hostile controls for that slice ARE authored here, as two more `Mutation`
  values, because a control that drove a copy of this file would prove nothing
  about this one:

    `upgrade-no-service`   the second package registers no service at all, so
                           after the upgrade the service the first package
                           installed has been removed and nothing replaced it.
                           The `ServiceControl` remove element is deliberately
                           left in place, so the defect is attributable to the
                           missing registration alone.

    `upgrade-wipes-state`  the second package deletes the contents of the four
                           state directories on install. It is authored as
                           `RemoveFile` on a component the upgrade certainly
                           installs, NOT by weakening the four retained-state
                           components: those are `NeverOverwrite`, so with
                           `HKLM\SOFTWARE\Tesserafin` still present from the
                           first install the upgrade SKIPS them and anything
                           authored inside them never runs. A control that
                           cannot fire is not a control.

  The other three W4-A4 controls are deliberately NOT authored here. "The second
  package keeps the first's `tesserafin.exe` bytes" is a property of what the
  builder is handed, not of this file, so it is a stage the probe supplies. A
  differing `UpgradeCode` and a `ServiceControl` that starts the service are both
  produced by editing the built MSI's own tables, for two reasons: a second
  `UpgradeCode` string in this file is RED to `ci/windows/w4/msi-controls.py` and
  so is `Start="install"` anywhere in it, and installing either one would create
  the second product the W4-A4 ruling forbids inventing, or roll back to 1603 and
  hide every other outcome behind one identity problem the way W0 §5.2 measured.

  What this authoring deliberately does NOT claim:

    * it is NOT a bit-identical MSI. W0 §5.6 measured that and accepted a
      bounded exception; W4-A0 makes no reproducibility claim at all;
    * it is NOT signed, and carries no certificate reference;
    * it does NOT start the service. W0 §10 is explicit that a fresh
      installation leaves the service "installed and enabled but not started";
    * it does NOT implement the rest of the §4 table. The 120 s stop timeout and
      the Event Log row are neither authored here nor measured, and W4-A2 rules
      on neither. The recovery row is the only one this slice adds;
    * it registers no Event Log source as a separate product;
    * it claims nothing about upgrade or repair beyond what `MajorUpgrade`
      emits by default.

  The `UpgradeCode` below is FROZEN by the W4-A1 owner ruling on #234. It is
  the GUID W4-A0 already authored, ratified as the 1.1 identity rather than
  re-minted:

    0f0c9f4e-1c5a-4b8e-9a3d-6d1f2b7c8e05

  ordinal, lowercase, no braces, exactly those bytes. It is the one value a
  later slice cannot change without stranding machines that already have the
  product installed, which is why it is pinned in the authoring and never
  generated per build. `ci/windows/w4/msi-controls.py` reddens any other GUID,
  and reddens these same digits written in a different case or wrapped in
  braces.

  The freeze reaches that one string and nothing else. The product name
  `Tesserafin Server`, the manufacturer `Tesserafin project` and the four
  retained-state component GUIDs are still only the identity this skeleton
  happened to build with; W4-A1 rules on none of them, and each still needs an
  explicit decision before 1.1 ships.

  Preprocessor inputs, all supplied by `ci/windows/w4/build-msi.ps1`:

    StageRoot   the extracted W2 package directory - the accepted layout,
                acquired through the frozen W2-A2 assembler, never restated
    HarvestRoot the same layout with the two files below held back, so that the
                harvest cannot deliver the server executable a second time
    Version     the MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH the commit already declares in
                SharedVersion.cs, the same source the assembler reads
    Mutation    `none` for the real package. Every other value builds a
                DELIBERATELY BROKEN package for one hostile control - four from
                W4-A0 over containment, the argument list and the uninstall, and
                three from W4-A2 over the recovery policy - and exists
                so those controls drive THIS authoring rather than a second
                copy of it written for the test - the same reason the frozen
                W2-A2 assembler carries its own PACK-ONLY parameter set.
                `ci/windows/w4/msi-controls.py` asserts the hosted acceptance
                build never passes anything but `none`.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:util="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/util">

  <?ifndef Mutation ?>
    <?define Mutation = "none" ?>
  <?endif ?>

  <!-- W4-A0 hostile control 1: "MSI does not contain tesserafin.exe". The
       component and the service registration are left intact so the control
       reddens containment and nothing else - a mutation that also removed the
       service would redden four predicates at once and be attributable to
       none of them. -->
  <?if $(var.Mutation) = "no-exe" ?>
    <?define ServerExeFileName = "tesserafin-w4control.exe" ?>
  <?else ?>
    <?define ServerExeFileName = "tesserafin.exe" ?>
  <?endif ?>

  <!-- W4-A3 (#234): the W0 §9.3 ACL contract, written out in full rather than
       assembled from parts. Every variant below is a complete SDDL DACL, so
       `ci/windows/w4/msi-controls.py` can parse each one and say what it grants,
       and a reviewer can read one string and know the answer. The four ACL
       mutations are the ruling's own hostile controls, each changing ONE thing
       about the contract.

       Administrators and SYSTEM carry Full in EVERY variant, including the
       mutants. That is deliberate: none of these controls is about the
       administrative rights, and a mutant that also dropped them would redden
       predicates it did not declare and be attributable to nothing. -->

  <!-- W4-A3-R2 (#234). TWO descriptors under `%ProgramData%`, and the reason
       there are two is a measurement rather than a preference.

       W4-A3 authored ONE, on `%ProgramData%\Tesserafin\`, with every ACE
       `OICI`, on the reading that the four state directories W0 §9.1 names
       would inherit it. Run 34502732425 read the live ACLs back and they had
       not. The parent came back

         D:P(A;OICI;FA;;;SY)(A;OICI;FA;;;BA)(A;OICI;0x1301bf;;;NT SERVICE)

       and every one of `config`, `data`, `cache` and `log` came back

         D:AI(A;OICIID;FA;;;SY)(A;OICIID;FA;;;BA)(A;OICIIOID;GA;;;CO)
            (A;OICIID;0x1200a9;;;BU)(A;CIID;DCLCRPCR;;;BU)

       which is `%ProgramData%`'s OWN default, every ACE flagged `ID`. Windows
       Installer sets the descriptor PROTECTED and without SE_DACL_AUTO_INHERITED,
       so the auto-inherit pass never runs and a directory that already exists
       keeps what it had. That last ACE is `BUILTIN\Users` at mask `0x116`: add
       file, add subdirectory, write EA, write attributes. It is exactly the
       "any authenticated user can create files under `%ProgramData%`" grant W0
       §9.2 warns about, and it was live on the directory holding the database.

       So a grant is only where it is authored. Every directory in the operator
       tree carries its own `PermissionEx`, and none of them relies on a parent.

       The intermediate `%ProgramData%\Tesserafin\Server` gets the same
       descriptor as the four. W0 §9.3's table does not name it, but it is the
       same defect: without one it keeps `%ProgramData%`'s `Users` write. A
       protected parent does not save it either, because `Users` hold
       SeChangeNotifyPrivilege by default and bypass-traverse-checking takes the
       access decision straight to the leaf.

       Administrators and SYSTEM carry Full in EVERY variant below, mutants
       included. None of the controls is about the administrative rights, and a
       mutant that dropped them would redden predicates it did not declare and
       be attributable to nothing.

       There is deliberately NO control that removes the `P`. The same run
       measured that `MsiLockPermissionsEx` writes a PROTECTED DACL whatever the
       SDDL asks for: `InstallFolderSddl` is authored `D:` and comes back `D:P`,
       and so did the `acl-not-protected` mutant that W4-A3-R2 withdrew. A
       control that cannot fail is not a control, and it graded "expected red but
       green". `dataRootInheritanceBroken` is still measured and still asserted;
       it simply is not falsifiable from this authoring. -->

  <!-- `%ProgramData%\Tesserafin\`, the directory W0 §9.3 breaks inheritance at.
       Three ACEs and no fourth: no `Users`, `Authenticated Users` or `Everyone`
       ACE at all, which is a stronger statement than the "no inherited write"
       §9.3 asks for. It carries no mutation of its own since W4-A3-R2, because
       the controls that were authored here now drive the descriptor the four
       state directories actually get. -->
  <?define DataRootSddl = "D:P(A;OICI;0x1f01ff;;;BA)(A;OICI;0x1f01ff;;;SY)(A;OICI;0x1301bf;;;S-1-5-80-761762137-1691453069-3789821951-3290391601-3361247659)" ?>

  <!-- The descriptor every directory in the operator tree gets: the four state
       directories W0 §9.3 names, and `Server` above them. `NT SERVICE\Tesserafin`
       Modify is the row §9.3 states for the four. -->
  <?if $(var.Mutation) = "acl-users-write" ?>
    <!-- W4-A3 hostile control: the state directories hand `Users` Modify. The
         descriptor is still protected and still grants the service account, so
         a gate that only asked whether inheritance was broken, or only what the
         service could do, would call this package correct. It reddens the
         `Users` row on the four state directories AND on `Server`, because one
         descriptor is what every one of them gets. -->
    <?define StateDirSddl = "D:P(A;OICI;0x1f01ff;;;BA)(A;OICI;0x1f01ff;;;SY)(A;OICI;0x1301bf;;;S-1-5-80-761762137-1691453069-3789821951-3290391601-3361247659)(A;OICI;0x1301bf;;;BU)" ?>
  <?elseif $(var.Mutation) = "acl-no-service-grant" ?>
    <!-- W4-A3 hostile control: the protected descriptor grants the service
         account nothing, so `NT SERVICE\Tesserafin` has no Modify on any of the
         four state directories. W0 §9.2 measured that the default
         `%ProgramData%` ACL happens to be permissive enough for the service to
         run anyway, which is exactly why this cannot be graded by starting it. -->
    <?define StateDirSddl = "D:P(A;OICI;0x1f01ff;;;BA)(A;OICI;0x1f01ff;;;SY)" ?>
  <?else ?>
    <?define StateDirSddl = "D:P(A;OICI;0x1f01ff;;;BA)(A;OICI;0x1f01ff;;;SY)(A;OICI;0x1301bf;;;S-1-5-80-761762137-1691453069-3789821951-3290391601-3361247659)" ?>
  <?endif ?>

  <!-- The real descriptor for INSTALLFOLDER. The service account's ACE is the
       row W0 §9.3 is about: read and execute, and no write bit, so the service
       cannot rewrite its own binaries or its own FFmpeg. The three rows before
       it are the ones every %ProgramFiles% directory already has, and they are
       authored here because W4-A3-R1 (#234) measured that they are NOT
       inherited.

       W4-A3 first authored this as the service ACE alone, on the reading that
       an unprotected descriptor ADDS an ACE and leaves what the parent grants
       Administrators and SYSTEM in place. Run 34500789866 measured otherwise:
       the standard sequence runs CreateFolders before InstallFiles, and two
       seconds into InstallFinalize the first payload file answered

         Product: Tesserafin Server, Error 1310. Error writing to file:
         D:\a\_temp\w4a0\prefix\none\ATL.dll. System error 0. Verify that
         you have access to that directory.

       (the installer writes a double hyphen where the comma is above; an XML
       comment cannot carry one)

       rolling the install back to 1603. What that proves, whatever Windows
       Installer does with the protection flag, is that the DACL the object ends
       up with is EXACTLY the SDDL authored here and nothing else. So the
       descriptor states the whole of it.

       It is still written WITHOUT `P`. W0 §9.3 breaks inheritance at
       `%ProgramData%\Tesserafin\` and nowhere else, so that is what this file
       asks for; whether Windows Installer protects it anyway is now read back
       off the live object and reported rather than assumed.

       One thing improves as a consequence. Under the disposable prefix the
       Administrators, SYSTEM and `Users` rows used to be inherited from the
       runner's temp tree, so grading them would have graded RUNNER_TEMP. They
       are authored now, so they are the package's own statement and the hosted
       proof grades all three. -->
  <?if $(var.Mutation) = "acl-install-writable" ?>
    <!-- W4-A3 hostile control 4: the service account gets Modify under
         INSTALLFOLDER, so it can rewrite its own binaries and its own FFmpeg.
         Read and execute still hold, so the control reddens the write half
         alone. -->
    <?define InstallFolderSddl = "D:(A;OICI;0x1f01ff;;;BA)(A;OICI;0x1f01ff;;;SY)(A;OICI;0x1200a9;;;BU)(A;OICI;0x1301bf;;;S-1-5-80-761762137-1691453069-3789821951-3290391601-3361247659)" ?>
  <?else ?>
    <?define InstallFolderSddl = "D:(A;OICI;0x1f01ff;;;BA)(A;OICI;0x1f01ff;;;SY)(A;OICI;0x1200a9;;;BU)(A;OICI;0x1200a9;;;S-1-5-80-761762137-1691453069-3789821951-3290391601-3361247659)" ?>
  <?endif ?>

  <Package Name="Tesserafin Server"
           Manufacturer="Tesserafin project"
           Version="$(var.Version)"
           UpgradeCode="0f0c9f4e-1c5a-4b8e-9a3d-6d1f2b7c8e05"
           Scope="perMachine"
           Compressed="yes">

    <!-- W0 §10 names `MajorUpgrade` as what makes the upgrade deterministic and
         the downgrade refused. It is present because a package without it
         installs a second product side by side, which would be a worse default
         than no statement at all. W4-A0 exercises neither path and claims
         neither. -->
    <MajorUpgrade DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of [ProductName] is already installed." />

    <!-- mszip, not the LZX default. The payload is the whole self-contained
         server plus the Web tree plus the FFmpeg runtime - thousands of files
         and hundreds of megabytes - and the default compression level turns a
         packaging step into the longest step in the job. Compression is not a
         property this slice measures. -->
    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" CompressionLevel="mszip" />

    <!-- W0 §9.1. Everything under INSTALLFOLDER is package-owned and replaced
         wholesale; everything under DATAFOLDER is operator-owned and never
         touched by the package. Both are public (upper-case) directory
         properties, so a caller can redirect either from the msiexec command
         line - which is how the hosted job installs under a disposable prefix
         instead of the runner's real %ProgramFiles%. -->
    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramFiles64Folder">
      <Directory Id="TesserafinProgramFiles" Name="Tesserafin">
        <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="Server" />
      </Directory>
    </StandardDirectory>

    <StandardDirectory Id="CommonAppDataFolder">
      <Directory Id="TesserafinProgramData" Name="Tesserafin">
        <Directory Id="DATAFOLDER" Name="Server">
          <Directory Id="DataConfigFolder" Name="config" />
          <Directory Id="DataDataFolder" Name="data" />
          <Directory Id="DataCacheFolder" Name="cache" />
          <Directory Id="DataLogFolder" Name="log" />
        </Directory>
      </Directory>
    </StandardDirectory>

    <!-- The package-owned payload, harvested from the accepted W2 layout rather
         than enumerated here. An authored File element per member would be a
         second statement of the layout that could disagree with the archive,
         and there are thousands of them.

         The harvest reads HarvestRoot, which `ci/windows/w4/build-msi.ps1`
         materialises as the accepted stage MINUS two files. It is a separate
         directory rather than an Exclude list because the harvest is a LINKER
         behaviour: an exclude that did not match would deliver the executable
         twice into the same directory and fail an ICE, and it would fail on the
         runner rather than anywhere it could be checked first.

         `tesserafin.exe` is held back because it is authored explicitly below:
         the service registration has to live on the component that owns the
         executable, and a harvested component cannot carry one. It is still
         sourced from the stage, so the harvested payload and the authored
         executable cannot come from different packages.

         `tesserafin-server-service.ps1` is held back because W0 §6 gives it to
         the portable ZIP as a convenience over the same §4 contract and is
         explicit that it "is **not** a second installer". Shipping it inside
         the MSI would put a second registration path on a machine that already
         has the packaged one. -->
    <ComponentGroup Id="PackagePayload" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <Files Include="$(var.HarvestRoot)\**" />
    </ComponentGroup>

    <ComponentGroup Id="ServerHost" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <Component Id="ServerExecutable" Guid="3c7a1f92-0b64-4e2d-8c19-5a4e7d0b6f31">
        <File Id="TesserafinServerExe"
              Name="$(var.ServerExeFileName)"
              Source="$(var.StageRoot)\tesserafin.exe"
              KeyPath="yes" />

        <!-- W4-A3 (#234): the W0 §9.3 grant on INSTALLFOLDER itself. It hangs
             off a CreateFolder rather than off the File above because the
             contract is about the DIRECTORY: the payload is thousands of
             harvested files in components of their own, and an ACE on one file
             would say nothing about the tree beside it. Windows Installer
             applies MsiLockPermissionsEx during CreateFolders, which the
             standard sequence runs before InstallFiles, so the delivered tree
             inherits the OICI ACE as it is written rather than being fixed up
             afterwards. -->
        <CreateFolder>
          <PermissionEx Sddl="$(var.InstallFolderSddl)" />
        </CreateFolder>

        <!-- W0 §4, the service contract, stated once and in full.

             `Start="auto"` plus `ServiceConfig/@DelayedAutoStart` is the
             "Automatic (Delayed Start)" row. This one is the CORE ServiceConfig
             element and it stays: W0 has been installing with it since W4-A0 and
             the delayed-autostart half of section 4 is the half that works. WiX
             answers WIX1149 for it - the Windows Installer SDK documents parts of
             it as not working as expected - so the hosted proof does not take the
             authoring's word for it: it reads `DelayedAutostart` back out of the
             service's own registry key after the install. It is unrelated to
             util:ServiceConfig below, which is the extension element that replaced
             the FAILURE-ACTIONS half after it answered 1939.

             There is deliberately NO `Start="install"` on ServiceControl. W0
             §5.2 recorded that starting the service inside the transaction
             failed with 1920 and rolled the whole install back to 1603, hiding
             every other outcome behind one identity problem; W0 §10 wants the
             service installed and enabled but not started anyway.

             The `webdir` and `ffmpeg` arguments (each spelled with two leading
             hyphens, which an XML comment cannot contain) are always explicit,
             exactly as the Linux unit is, so the service can never fall back to a PATH encoder or a
             stale web directory. The `nowebclient` argument is never used. -->
        <?if $(var.Mutation) = "no-service-flag" ?>
          <!-- W4-A0 hostile control 2: the service binPath lacks the `service` switch. -->
          <ServiceInstall Id="TesserafinService"
                          Name="Tesserafin"
                          DisplayName="Tesserafin Server"
                          Description="Tesserafin media server. Manage it at http://localhost:8096."
                          Type="ownProcess"
                          Start="auto"
                          ErrorControl="normal"
                          Account="NT SERVICE\Tesserafin"
                          Vital="yes"
                          Arguments="--configdir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]config&quot; --datadir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]data&quot; --cachedir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]cache&quot; --logdir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]log&quot; --webdir &quot;[INSTALLFOLDER]web&quot; --ffmpeg &quot;[INSTALLFOLDER]ffmpeg\bin\ffmpeg.exe&quot;">
            <ServiceConfig DelayedAutoStart="yes" OnInstall="yes" OnReinstall="yes" />
            <util:ServiceConfig FirstFailureActionType="restart"
                                SecondFailureActionType="restart"
                                ThirdFailureActionType="none"
                                RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="60"
                                ResetPeriodInDays="1" />
          </ServiceInstall>
        <?elseif $(var.Mutation) = "upgrade-no-service" ?>
          <!-- W4-A4 hostile control: the upgrade registers NO service. This
               branch authors no ServiceInstall at all, so after MajorUpgrade has
               removed the first package - taking its service with it, through
               the ServiceControl element below, which this control deliberately
               leaves in place - nothing registers one again. The state
               directories, the payload and the descriptors are all untouched, so
               the control is attributable to the missing registration and to the
               consequences that follow from it. -->
        <?elseif $(var.Mutation) = "no-path-flags" ?>
          <!-- W4-A0 hostile control 3: the binPath lacks `ffmpeg` and `webdir`. -->
          <ServiceInstall Id="TesserafinService"
                          Name="Tesserafin"
                          DisplayName="Tesserafin Server"
                          Description="Tesserafin media server. Manage it at http://localhost:8096."
                          Type="ownProcess"
                          Start="auto"
                          ErrorControl="normal"
                          Account="NT SERVICE\Tesserafin"
                          Vital="yes"
                          Arguments="--service --configdir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]config&quot; --datadir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]data&quot; --cachedir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]cache&quot; --logdir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]log&quot;">
            <ServiceConfig DelayedAutoStart="yes" OnInstall="yes" OnReinstall="yes" />
            <util:ServiceConfig FirstFailureActionType="restart"
                                SecondFailureActionType="restart"
                                ThirdFailureActionType="none"
                                RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="60"
                                ResetPeriodInDays="1" />
          </ServiceInstall>
        <?else ?>
          <ServiceInstall Id="TesserafinService"
                          Name="Tesserafin"
                          DisplayName="Tesserafin Server"
                          Description="Tesserafin media server. Manage it at http://localhost:8096."
                          Type="ownProcess"
                          Start="auto"
                          ErrorControl="normal"
                          Account="NT SERVICE\Tesserafin"
                          Vital="yes"
                          Arguments="--service --configdir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]config&quot; --datadir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]data&quot; --cachedir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]cache&quot; --logdir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]log&quot; --webdir &quot;[INSTALLFOLDER]web&quot; --ffmpeg &quot;[INSTALLFOLDER]ffmpeg\bin\ffmpeg.exe&quot;">
            <ServiceConfig DelayedAutoStart="yes" OnInstall="yes" OnReinstall="yes" />
            <!-- W0 section 4, the recovery row: restart after 60 s on the first and
                 the second failure, and NO action on the third, so a crash loop is
                 visible rather than hidden.

                 W4-A2-R1 (#234) replaced the core ServiceConfigFailureActions
                 element that used to stand here. That element writes the
                 MsiServiceConfigFailureActions table, which the MsiConfigureServices
                 standard action applies under InstallFinalize - and on this package
                 that action answered MSI error 1939, "Service 'Tesserafin Server'
                 (Tesserafin) could not be configured", which rolled the whole
                 install back to 1603. The owner ruling accepted that diagnosis and
                 authorised this element instead.

                 util:ServiceConfig is WixToolset.Util.wixext, pinned by exact
                 version on the `wix` command line by ci/windows/w4/build-msi.ps1 -
                 the first WiX extension this packaging takes. It is NOT a table
                 row: it schedules a deferred custom action that opens the service
                 and calls ChangeServiceConfig2W(SERVICE_CONFIG_FAILURE_ACTIONS)
                 itself, after InstallServices has created it, with the SERVICE_START
                 access right SC_ACTION_RESTART requires.

                 The units are the EXTENSION'S, not the SCM's, and the conversion is
                 the custom action's: RestartServiceDelayInSeconds is multiplied by
                 1000 into SC_ACTION.Delay, so 60 is the 60000 ms W0 section 4 asks
                 for, and ResetPeriodInDays is multiplied by 86400 into
                 SERVICE_FAILURE_ACTIONS.dwResetPeriod, so 1 is section 4's 86400 s.
                 There is ONE delay attribute and it feeds every restart entry -
                 which is why the delay control below reddens the first and the
                 second predicate together rather than one of them.

                 The third entry is load-bearing and is not decoration. The SCM
                 repeats the LAST configured action for every failure beyond the
                 length of the array, so a two-entry array of restarts restarts
                 forever - the exact outcome W0 section 4 refuses. The custom action
                 always writes exactly three SC_ACTIONs and gives a non-restart entry
                 a zero delay, so ThirdFailureActionType="none" is the explicit
                 SC_ACTION_NONE that makes the loop stop and become visible, and
                 `third-action-restart` below is the control that proves this
                 authoring is what produces it.

                 Nothing here is graded from the authoring. The hosted proof reads
                 the failure policy back out of the Service Control Manager with
                 QueryServiceConfig2W after the install, and grades THAT. -->
            <?if $(var.Mutation) = "no-util-config" ?>
              <!-- W4-A2 hostile control 1: no util:ServiceConfig authored at all,
                   so the extension's custom action is never scheduled for this
                   service. The SCM then takes its default, which is to do nothing on
                   any failure, and W0 section 4's first and second restart never
                   happen. -->
            <?elseif $(var.Mutation) = "delay-not-60s" ?>
              <!-- W4-A2 hostile control 2: the restart delay is not 60 s. Both
                   failures are still restarts, and still authored ones, so a gate
                   that only asked which ACTION the SCM recorded would call this
                   correct; the delay is the half that changed. util:ServiceConfig
                   carries ONE delay for every restart entry, so this one defect has
                   two visible consequences - the first AND the second predicate -
                   and it is declared with both, the way `no-exe` is declared with
                   three. -->
              <util:ServiceConfig FirstFailureActionType="restart"
                                  SecondFailureActionType="restart"
                                  ThirdFailureActionType="none"
                                  RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="1"
                                  ResetPeriodInDays="1" />
            <?elseif $(var.Mutation) = "third-action-restart" ?>
              <!-- W4-A2 hostile control 3: the third failure restarts, so the crash
                   loop W0 section 4 wants visible is hidden behind an indefinite
                   restart. Every other row of the policy is correct, which is what
                   makes this control attributable to the third entry alone. -->
              <util:ServiceConfig FirstFailureActionType="restart"
                                  SecondFailureActionType="restart"
                                  ThirdFailureActionType="restart"
                                  RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="60"
                                  ResetPeriodInDays="1" />
            <?else ?>
              <util:ServiceConfig FirstFailureActionType="restart"
                                  SecondFailureActionType="restart"
                                  ThirdFailureActionType="none"
                                  RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="60"
                                  ResetPeriodInDays="1" />
            <?endif ?>
          </ServiceInstall>
        <?endif ?>

        <!-- W4-A4 hostile control: the upgrade destroys operator state.

             It is authored HERE, on the component that owns the executable and
             that every install of this package therefore installs, and NOT by
             taking `Permanent` or `NeverOverwrite` off the four retained-state
             components below. Both of those would be inert. `RemoveFolder`
             removes only an EMPTY directory, so a state directory holding a
             sentinel survives a non-permanent component anyway; and a
             `NeverOverwrite` component whose registry key path already exists -
             which it does, because the first install created it - is SKIPPED
             outright on the upgrade, so anything authored inside it never runs.

             `On="install"` puts this in the RemoveFiles standard action, which
             the standard sequence runs before InstallFiles, so the sentinels are
             gone by the time the upgrade has finished. The DIRECTORIES survive,
             because the retained components below are still Permanent - which is
             what makes this control redden the sentinel predicates and not the
             directory ones. -->
        <?if $(var.Mutation) = "upgrade-wipes-state" ?>
          <RemoveFile Id="W4A4WipeConfig" Name="*" On="install" Directory="DataConfigFolder" />
          <RemoveFile Id="W4A4WipeData" Name="*" On="install" Directory="DataDataFolder" />
          <RemoveFile Id="W4A4WipeCache" Name="*" On="install" Directory="DataCacheFolder" />
          <RemoveFile Id="W4A4WipeLog" Name="*" On="install" Directory="DataLogFolder" />
        <?endif ?>

        <!-- Removing the service is a property of the package, not of a custom
             action that could be skipped. W4-A0 hostile control 4 - "uninstall
             leaves the service registered" - is this element's absence. -->
        <?if $(var.Mutation) != "no-service-remove" ?>
          <ServiceControl Id="TesserafinServiceControl"
                          Name="Tesserafin"
                          Stop="both"
                          Remove="uninstall"
                          Wait="yes" />
        <?endif ?>
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>

    <!-- W4-A3 (#234), reshaped by W4-A3-R2. One component per directory in the
         operator tree, each carrying that directory's own security descriptor.

         W4-A3 authored one descriptor, on `%ProgramData%\Tesserafin\`, and
         relied on OICI inheritance to carry it down. Run 34502732425 measured
         that it does not: Windows Installer writes the DACL PROTECTED and
         without SE_DACL_AUTO_INHERITED, so the auto-inherit pass never runs and
         a directory that already exists keeps whatever it had. All four state
         directories came back carrying `%ProgramData%`'s own default, `Users`
         write bits included. A grant is only where it is authored, so every
         directory authors one.

         None of these components is `Permanent` and none is `NeverOverwrite`,
         and the four retained-state components below deliberately are. The
         difference is what each one owns. Those four own operator DATA, which
         an ordinary uninstall must keep. These own security descriptors, which
         are package policy: they have to be re-applied by every install and
         every repair, and a `NeverOverwrite` component whose key path already
         existed would be SKIPPED, taking its CreateFolder and therefore its
         descriptor with it. Uninstall removes these registry values and then
         fails to remove the directories, because the four Permanent components
         below keep them from being empty, so the directories and their
         descriptors both survive: the behaviour W0 §10 asks for.

         `Server` is in the list although W0 §9.3's table does not name it. It
         is the same defect: without a descriptor it keeps `%ProgramData%`'s
         `Users` write, and the protected parent does not save it, because
         `Users` hold SeChangeNotifyPrivilege by default and
         bypass-traverse-checking takes the access decision straight to the leaf.

         The registry key paths are the same shape the retained components use,
         for the same reason: a component whose key path is a directory is
         evaluated by directory existence, which makes its installed state
         depend on something the operator can change. Nothing reads the values. -->
    <ComponentGroup Id="OperatorTreePermissions">
      <Component Id="DataRootAcl" Directory="TesserafinProgramData" Guid="2f6c8b41-9a03-4d17-b5e8-7c40e19d3a62">
        <CreateFolder>
          <PermissionEx Sddl="$(var.DataRootSddl)" />
        </CreateFolder>
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="Software\Tesserafin\Server\Acl" Name="dataRoot" Value="applied" Type="string" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="ServerDirAcl" Directory="DATAFOLDER" Guid="8d1a47f6-2c95-4b03-9e7a-1f62d05c8b41">
        <CreateFolder>
          <PermissionEx Sddl="$(var.StateDirSddl)" />
        </CreateFolder>
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="Software\Tesserafin\Server\Acl" Name="server" Value="applied" Type="string" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="ConfigDirAcl" Directory="DataConfigFolder" Guid="c40e7b19-6a82-4d55-b1f3-9e2740a6c83d">
        <CreateFolder>
          <PermissionEx Sddl="$(var.StateDirSddl)" />
        </CreateFolder>
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="Software\Tesserafin\Server\Acl" Name="config" Value="applied" Type="string" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="DataDirAcl" Directory="DataDataFolder" Guid="71b93de4-05fa-4c68-8d27-3b5e6f014a92">
        <CreateFolder>
          <PermissionEx Sddl="$(var.StateDirSddl)" />
        </CreateFolder>
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="Software\Tesserafin\Server\Acl" Name="data" Value="applied" Type="string" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="CacheDirAcl" Directory="DataCacheFolder" Guid="e6274f0b-93d1-4a87-b52c-08fa1d6e3947">
        <CreateFolder>
          <PermissionEx Sddl="$(var.StateDirSddl)" />
        </CreateFolder>
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="Software\Tesserafin\Server\Acl" Name="cache" Value="applied" Type="string" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="LogDirAcl" Directory="DataLogFolder" Guid="3a58c1d7-4e60-49b2-a0f8-62d7395bc108">
        <CreateFolder>
          <PermissionEx Sddl="$(var.StateDirSddl)" />
        </CreateFolder>
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="Software\Tesserafin\Server\Acl" Name="log" Value="applied" Type="string" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>

    <!-- W0 §9.1 and §10: the operator-owned directories. They are created by
         the package so that "uninstall does not delete them" is a measurable
         statement rather than a vacuous one, and they are `Permanent` so that
         the retained-data policy is expressed IN THE PACKAGE - an ordinary
         uninstall cannot remove them, and no custom action has to be trusted
         not to.

         `NeverOverwrite` keeps a reinstall or a repair from touching a
         directory whose contents belong to the operator.

         The registry KeyPath is the W0 §5.2 probe's shape: a component whose
         KeyPath is a directory is evaluated by directory existence, which makes
         `Permanent` and `NeverOverwrite` behave unpredictably. Nothing reads
         these values; they exist to give each component a stable identity. -->
    <ComponentGroup Id="RetainedState" Directory="DATAFOLDER">
      <Component Id="RetainedConfig" Directory="DataConfigFolder" Guid="9d2b7c05-4a18-4f63-b0e7-2c8a51d94f76" Permanent="yes" NeverOverwrite="yes">
        <CreateFolder />
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="Software\Tesserafin\Server\RetainedState" Name="config" Value="retained" Type="string" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="RetainedData" Directory="DataDataFolder" Guid="4e81a63f-7d29-4c05-9b6a-8f3e0d27c1b4" Permanent="yes" NeverOverwrite="yes">
        <CreateFolder />
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="Software\Tesserafin\Server\RetainedState" Name="data" Value="retained" Type="string" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="RetainedCache" Directory="DataCacheFolder" Guid="b7f04c18-3e5d-4a92-8c71-0d6b2f9e5a83" Permanent="yes" NeverOverwrite="yes">
        <CreateFolder />
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="Software\Tesserafin\Server\RetainedState" Name="cache" Value="retained" Type="string" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="RetainedLog" Directory="DataLogFolder" Guid="5a3d9e26-8b47-4f10-a6c2-1e7f4b0d38c9" Permanent="yes" NeverOverwrite="yes">
        <CreateFolder />
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="Software\Tesserafin\Server\RetainedState" Name="log" Value="retained" Type="string" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>

    <Feature Id="Main" Title="Tesserafin Server" Level="1">
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="PackagePayload" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="ServerHost" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="OperatorTreePermissions" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="RetainedState" />
    </Feature>
  </Package>
</Wix>
